    # C-06 · Macroeconomic · Macrotrend, Multi-region · megatrend: Economic Growth Under Constraints · first observed 2022
    Driver(
        id="consumer_r06", force="Consumer",
        name="Cost-of-living squeeze, round two: energy-shock inflation, entrenched value-seeking and wallet shift to services",
        description="The squeeze did not end with the 2024-25 rate cuts: after the Middle East war began in late February 2026, euro-area inflation returned to 3.3% in August 2026 (energy +14.3%) and the ECB raised rates on 11 June 2026 citing the war. BCG's June 2026 survey finds 53% of Europeans worried about daily finances (40% in 2024) and expects the pullback to persist even if conditions improve; McKinsey expects more than three-quarters of consumers worldwide to keep trading down. HCB's own Q2 2026 shows the new form of the squeeze: price -0.5%, volume +2.1%. Two constraints tighten it: FMCG shelf inflation is only 1.1% (NIQ EU5, 13 weeks to May 2026) against direct-material inflation in the high single digits, and shrinkflation-disclosure laws (France since July 2024, Italy since April 2025, Austria since April 2026; the Bremen court's Milka ruling of May 2026) remove covert downsizing as a lever. Retail's share of EU private consumption fell for a fourth year (31.9%) as wallets move to services and travel. Premium bifurcation holds: NIQ Premium+ is 28.2% of Western European FMCG value and growing 4.1%.",
        direction="Contraction", probability=5, start_year=2025,
        gp1_pct_affected=0.2, peak_year=2028, diffusion_curve="front_loaded", uncertainty=3,
        confidence="High",
        category_exposure=_categories(2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4, 4, 5, 3, 4, 3, 3, 2),
        regional_exposure=_regions(5, 4, 3, 3),
        vc_epicentre="Consumer",
        sources=[
            {"title": "Eurostat flash HICP August 2026", "url": "https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/web/products-euro-indicators/w/2-01092026-ap", "tier": "S", "published": "Sep 2026"},
            {"title": "ECB monetary policy decision 11 June 2026", "url": "https://www.ecb.europa.eu/press/pr/date/2026/html/ecb.mp260611~4d41bd5e83.en.html", "tier": "S", "published": "Jun 2026"},
            {"title": "McKinsey: State of the Consumer 2026", "url": "https://www.mckinsey.com/industries/consumer-packaged-goods/our-insights/state-of-consumer", "tier": "A", "published": "Jun 2026"},
            {"title": "NIQ: European retail in 2025 and 2026", "url": "https://nielseniq.com/global/en/insights/report/2026/european-retail-in-2025-and-2026/", "tier": "A", "published": "Aug 2026"},
            {"title": "NIQ EU5 Inflation Barometer", "url": "https://nielseniq.com/global/en/news-center/2026/great-britain-leads-europes-fmcg-inflation-as-niq-launches-new-inflation-barometer/", "tier": "A", "published": "Aug 2026"},
            {"title": "DLA Piper: International shrinkflation guide", "url": "https://www.dlapiper.com/en/insights/topics/international-shrinkflation-guide", "tier": "A", "published": "2026"},
        ],
        analysis="Weight in the answer: The cost-of-living squeeze carries 0.69 of the 5.6 points, the third largest contribution of the 51 and 33 percent of the Consumer headwind. It is one of the broadest drivers in the base, present in every category and every region, and still reaches only 56 percent of the pool, because the categories that take trade-down first, laundry additives, fabric care, auto dishwash and fabric clean, are where it is most present, and the large hair colour cell is where it is least. Pass-through and overlap are the standard Consumer values.\n\nWhere it lands: Fabric clean Europe (0.11 points) and fabric clean North America (0.07 points) carry a quarter of the effect between them; 60 percent of it is European and 23 percent North American. Its category signature is the premium-format laundry lines: it accounts for 17 percent of the laundry additives contraction, 16 percent of fabric care and 15 percent of fabric finishers. In the European laundry cells it sits next to C-01 (private label) and in the North American ones next to C-34 (household formation) and G-15 (SNAP retrenchment); C-01 and this driver are one inflation complex and the overlap correction already discounts them together.\n\nWhen it lands: The most front-loaded of the five largest drivers: 83 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027 and 95 percent by 2028, the half-way point is 2026 and nothing is added after 2030. The second round is modelled as an energy-shock episode that fades if oil normalises in 2027 on the World Bank path, while the value-seeking habit it entrenches persists; that is why the ramp is steep and the peak early.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.52 to 0.81 points and it accounts for 10 percent of the portfolio band's variance. The width is the oil and rates path over 2026 and 2027, the same dependence E-12 (the feedstock shock) carries, so the two should be read as one oil-path exposure: a ceasefire or a longer war moves both. The observables are euro-area inflation (3.3 percent in August 2026, energy plus 14.3 percent), the ECB's path after the June 2026 hike and HCB's own price and volume prints, where Q2 2026 showed price minus 0.5 percent against volume plus 2.1 percent: two quarters of positive price with stable volume would argue for a smaller effect, a second energy leg for a larger one.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
    ),
