        analysis="Weight in the answer: Professional and bond-repair brands crossing into retail offset 0.12 of the 7.3 points of gross headwind, the fifth largest tailwind of the 18 and the largest tailwind in the Customer force, where it is 65 percent of a small total. It is a hair driver with a high Shift value that reaches only 25 percent of the pool, and the Customer force takes back a further 18 percent through the overlap correction it shares with K-03 (buyer power), K-13 (retail media) and K-12 (drugstores) on the same European hair cells.\n\nWhere it lands: Hair colour Europe (0.03 points) and hair care Europe (0.02 points) carry 42 percent of the effect; in hair colour Asia it offsets 14 percent of the cell's net contraction, its largest share of any cell. Europe holds 54 percent of the total and North America 23 percent. It offsets 7 percent of the hair care contraction, 6 percent of hair colour and 6 percent of hair styling. It is the expansion side of a channel whose contraction side is X-18 (professional channel restructuring): the crossover lifts the retail price ladder while the salon channel it comes from erodes, and the net of the pair is what the professional business shows.\n\nWhen it lands: An s-curve to 2030: 75 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 96 percent by 2029, the half-way point is 2028 and the effect is complete by 2030.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range is, relative to its size, as narrow as any tailwind's in the base, 0.10 to 0.14 points, because the crossover is documented brand by brand and its pace is bounded (L'Oréal Professional Products above EUR 5bn with 39 percent of sales outside salons, plus 12.6 percent like-for-like in H1 2026; Henkel's professional growth very strong in H1 2026). What the reader should hold against it is the capture caveat inside the driver: the crossover is led by L'Oréal PPD, Olaplex, K18 and Wella, so it raises category value that HCB rides through Olaplex and its own professional business rather than owns. The observables are PPD's outside-salon share and Olaplex's own retail trajectory inside HCB.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
