        analysis="Weight in the answer: The nature and supply-chain due-diligence stack carries 0.06 of the 5.6 points, the 42nd largest contribution of the 51 and 4 percent of the Government headwind. It reaches 33 percent of the pool, the surfactant-heavy categories where the pass-through from oleochemical suppliers lands, with a European regional profile because EUDR and CSDDD costs land on goods placed on the EU market; its Shift value is among the smallest in the base because the obligation bites on HCB's suppliers, not on HCB.\n\nWhere it lands: 82 percent of the effect is European; fabric clean Europe carries 26 percent of it (0.02 points) and hair care Europe 14 percent. Its largest category shares are hand dishwash (2 percent of the contraction) and hair care (2 percent), where surfactant intensity is highest. It shares the European cells with G-04 (packaging), G-16 (the Detergents Regulation) and G-02 (microplastics). The palm price premium that EUDR creates (USD 350 to 400 per tonne on crude palm kernel oil) is deliberately not here; it sits in E-01, and this driver carries only the due-diligence obligation itself: reference numbers under Regulation (EU) 2025/2650 and the CSDDD conduct duties.\n\nWhen it lands: Linear to 2030 from a dated start: EUDR applies from 30 December 2026 for large and medium operators, so 2027 is the first affected path year; CSDDD after Omnibus I applies from 26 July 2029 only above 5,000 employees and EUR 1.5bn turnover. 52 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2028, 95 percent by 2030 and the effect is complete by 2031.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range is narrow, 0.04 to 0.07 points; the dates are in law and the width is the pass-through premium, which varies with supplier compliance. The scope has been narrowing rather than widening (surfactants under HS 3402 and finished detergents and shampoos are out of EUDR scope; civil liability and mandatory transition plans were removed from CSDDD), so the observable is the reverse of the usual: a widening of scope, or a supplier compliance failure in the palm kernel chain at the December 2026 application date, would be the trigger to re-score. The Kunming-Montreal framework and TNFD are not company mandates and add nothing here.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
