        analysis="Weight in the answer: The post-IEEPA US tariff regime carries 0.05 of the 5.6 points, the 44th largest contribution of the 51 and 4 percent of the Government headwind. It is a North American cost driver of imported inputs and finished goods with only a residual presence elsewhere, so it reaches 18 percent of the pool; the Government pass-through and overlap correction are mid-range, and its small Shift value reflects a regime that Henkel calls manageable and that P&G and Church & Dwight book as a 2026 tailwind.\n\nWhere it lands: North America holds 59 percent of the effect and Europe 27 percent although Europe carries the same residual presence as the growth markets, because a residual presence on the region that holds half the pool outweighs the same presence on a fifth of it; fabric clean North America carries 20 percent of it. Its largest category shares are hair styling (2 percent of the contraction), where aerosol cans fall under the Section 232 metals tariff at 50 percent, and body care (1 percent). It sits on the North American cells with G-15 (SNAP retrenchment), G-13 (MoCRA and the state bans) and G-04 (packaging), the North American Government stack.\n\nWhen it lands: Front-loaded to 2027 with a long tail: 93 percent of the terminal effect is in the numbers by 2027 and the whole of it by 2029. The Supreme Court struck down the IEEPA tariffs on 20 February 2026, the Section 122 surcharge ran from 24 February to 24 July 2026 and the Section 301 tariffs of 10 or 12.5 percent on 60 trading partners were announced on 24 July 2026, so the regime the driver describes is already in place.\n\nWhat the band hinges on: Its own range runs from 0.03 to 0.07 points, the ordinary width for a driver of its size, and what sits inside it is policy volatility in both directions: the 2026 average effective rate of 6.6 percent is the highest since 1969, yet P&G booked a 40 basis point net tariff benefit to gross margin in its June 2026 quarter and Church & Dwight expects about USD 15m of refunds in H2 2026. The observables are the Tax Foundation's effective-rate tracker, the snap-back clauses in the EU regulation of 25 June 2026 that eliminated its duties on most US industrial goods, and the Section 301 investigation into German pharmaceutical pricing opened in June 2026; a re-escalation on the transatlantic route would argue for the upper end of the range, refunds landing as guided for the lower end.\n\nFigures: model attribution on the estimated HCB gross-profit mix, reconciled to a 2035 portfolio median of -5.6 percent (3 x 50,000 iterations, seed 42). Read as exposure if nobody acts, not as a forecast.",
